Installation instructions for BaseEnv

The file INSTALL contains generic installation instructions for programs using
autoconf and automake and using configure to build Makefiles. This file contains
information specific to BaseEnv

Building BaseEnv
================

To build BaseEnv, first run ./configure and then run make.
In the common case, configure BaseEnv by providing the compilers that are used
for MPI programs. For example, this works on my MacBook:

     configure CC=mpicc FC=mpifort CXX=mpic++

On some systems, no special compilers are needed. However, configure will prefer
GNU compilers, so you may want to specify the compilers. For example, on
the Delta and DeltaAI systems at NCSA, this configure line is used:

    configure CC=cc FC=fortran CXX=c++  CHECK!!!!

There are several features that you may want to consider. Enable options
are used to turn these on. For example,

--enable-cuda - Enable use of CUDA memory
--enable-hip  - Enable use of HIP memory
--enable-sycl - Enable use of SYCL memory

--enable-strict - If using the GNU compilers, enable verbose compiler warnings
                  about code usage, such as uninitialized variables

In some cases, the default paths (e.g., for include or library files) need
to be overridden for some feature. Use --with-xxx options to provide such
information. For example:

--with-cuda=path
--with-cuda-sdk=path
--with-hwlocdir=path

Finally, there are some standard configure options that are often used.
For example,

--prefix=path - Install into path

When configure runs, it will output the results of each test. Most of this can
be ignored unless there are problems. The BaseEnv configure outputs, at the end,
a summary of the major features it has found, particularly for hardware
configuration.

Creating the Libraries and Programs
===================================
Second, build the libraries and programs. Simply execute make:

    make

Testing the Libraries and Programs
==================================
Unfortunately, many systems do not provide a standard mpiexec program that
can run an arbitrary MPI program on a given number of processes.
Some of the library directories contain a "tests" subdirectory. These programs
can be built by cd'ing to that directory and running make. The directory
runscripts contains example batch and shell scripts that run the tests. You
will need to create your own scripts following these examples. Please consider
contributing your scripts to the repository.

Documentation
=============
Most of the documentation of the routines and programs is produced from the
source files with the program doctext, which was developed in the early 1990s
to produce documentation from structured comments in source files. Running
doctext requires a separate packate, sowing. The BaseEnv documentation
consists of man (nroff) files in the directory man, HTML files in the directory
www, and LaTeX files in the directory doc/tex.

